Choosing between reduceByKey and groupByKey is the classic Apache Spark interview question
because one is highly optimized, and the other is a notorious cluster-killer. 
While both group data by a specific key, the difference lies entirely in how they handle 
data shuffling across the network.

Technical Breakdown :- 
groupByKey (The Naive Approach): This method shuffles all the raw data across the network 
before doing any aggregation. If you have 10 million sales records for 'New York' distributed across your cluster, 
Spark will serialize all 10 million individual records, push them across the network,
and dump them onto a single worker node to be grouped.

reduceByKey (The Optimized Approach): This method performs a 'map-side combine'.
It aggregates the data locally on each individual worker node first. Instead of sending 
10 million raw records across the network, each worker node calculates its own local total for 'New York',
and then sends exactly one record—the local subtotal—across the network to be combined into a final grand total.

Buisness Context :- 
Network I/O is the most expensive and slowest operation in cloud computing.
Using groupByKey maxes out your network bandwidth, spikes cluster compute costs, and 
frequently causes pipelines to crash with Out-Of-Memory (OOM) errors because a single node cannot 
hold all the raw values for a heavily skewed key. reduceByKey prevents this, 
ensuring pipelines finish cheaply and reliably.

Design Decision :- 
In practice today, as a data engineer,
I rarely write either of these commands because they belong to the legacy RDD API.
We use the DataFrame API instead. When you write df.groupBy("city").sum("sales") 
in PySpark or Spark SQL, the Catalyst Optimizer is smart enough to automatically apply the optimized 
map-side combine logic of reduceByKey under the hood. 
However, understanding this mechanic is absolutely mandatory for debugging,
especially if an engineer writes a custom User Defined Aggregation Function (UDAF) that inadvertently 
forces Catalyst to fall back to a groupByKey execution plan.
